% Every binding pattern of a two-column predicate, asked by the queries the
% program itself contains, over a graph with a cycle and a dead end.  Each
% query's rows are sorted, and the results of successive queries are
% separated by a blank line.
edge(a, b).
edge(b, c).
edge(c, b).
edge(c, d).

path(X, Y) :-
    edge(X, Y).

path(X, Z) :-
    edge(X, Y),
    path(Y, Z).

% Free second column, free first column, both free, the same variable twice.
path(a, X)?
path(X, d)?
path(X, Y)?
path(X, X)?

% Both bound: a query that holds has one row of no values, and one that does
% not has no rows at all.  A query with rows stands between the two, since
% the empty row is otherwise the empty line that separates answers; its
% first column is `_`, and an anonymous variable is not an output column.
path(a, c)?
path(_, X)?
path(d, a)?
